Date: Wed, 12 May 93 21:17:42 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: AI: Synaptic Storage Requirements

> From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
> Another common assumption is that the effect of pre-synaptic
> neurons on a post-synaptic cell is a summation of their activations
> times the weights on their connections.  This is something I feel
> comfortable with, but it makes neurophysiologists shudder.

For example, "negative weights" work very differently in real life.
An inhibitory synapse can basically short out the dendrite at a
particular point (I think passive-potential hyperpolarization also
happens).  This will simply erase anything happening "upstream" on
the dendritic tree, while having little to no effect on activations
occurring elsewhere.  A dot product doesn't take this into account
at all.  I have no idea how much the brain's microcircuitry actually
makes use of this, but this particular approximation worries me.  A
lot of interesting things are done with inhibition.

> -Paul

   Eli   ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu

