Date: Thu, 13 May 1993 02:16:23 -0400
From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: GAMES: fun and growth.

Sasha says:
 
> My current opinion is: if you do not have enough intelligence to play
> these game fairly well - don't play; if you do have the intelligence,
> don't play anyway - use it for something more productive.
 
 Tim May paraphrases:
 
> "My current opinion is: if you do not have enough intelligence to
> write interesting messages to the list - don't write; if you do have
> the intelligence, don't write anyway - use it for something more
> productive."
  
  As the French say, "Comparison n'est pas reason" (or something like that).
I do agree that the second paragraph above is structurally equivalent to
the first, but this similarity doesn't apply to their validity; consider
statements "Sasha came from Russia" and "Timothy_C_May came from Russia".
The assertion of my statement was not in its structure, but the application
of it to the word "games".
 
  Not that I am against games - I just try to save my time for what brings
*me* more fun (= "is more productive").
 
  I would consider games an art form (in some of their aspects); if we
define art as something we consume for joy (as opposed to producing
something else), then we may distinguish artforms that we enjoy while
perceiving them (movies, culinary art, music), and after perceiving them
(game analysis, certain philosophical and mathematical constructs, etc.).
  Games may also be tools for personal development; in fact, since only
a few of all possible activities may be considered 'productive', and
since there seems to be no connection between usefullness of educational
techniques and their strengths, one may expect that the top zillion of
the most interesting and educationally useful activities would be
"unproductive" = games.
 
  Unfortunately, people seldom consciosly use games for self-development;
they rather use this ["usefullness"] argument to justify playing whatever
they feel like playing.
  I used to make some attempts to purposefully design games for teaching;
this included cooperative computer games where the player would *cooperate*
with the partner to reach the goal of the game (while most of existing
games 'teach' you to either work alone, or oppose every other game agent),
and games where players could dynamically modify the rules - both of these
fill our real life, but are almost untouched by the world of games.
  People prefer stable and simple rules though :-(
This allows them to live in the simple static VR of the game, and feel the
comfort and power they fail to achieve in the complex and volatile reality.
Which is very, very unextropian, among other things.

