> This one's a real result, not a floating fudge factor.  George Miller, 
> "The magical number seven, plus or minus two:  some limits on our 
> capacity for processing information".  _Psychological Review_ 63:81-97
> (1956).

Yes, however Miller's study had NOTHING to do with the speed with which 
people can learn into long-term memory (which was the original claim
being made for one bit per second, here).

I won't go over Miller in detail (it is a very short and readable
paper), suffice it to say that it was concerned with short-term retention
not long-term.

I don't know of any studies on the maximal learning rate, but given the
fact that a human is capable of being exposed to visual stimulus (such
as opening your eyes for a split second), and remembering the entire 
scene in full-color, I'd say the data rate has got to be pretty 
high!

The one-bit-per-second claim is not only not backed up by research, but
it is easily proven false by any human.

