Date: Thu, 13 May 93 10:29:55 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: AI: Synaptic Storage Requirements

Paul volleys:

> To confidently assume that our assumptions are correct and to begin
> calculating conclusions based upon them is precarious.  It is this
> that I'm pointing out - that we cannot make quantitative statements
> until we understand qualitatively what we are counting.  I'm claiming
> that we don't have this understanding yet.

Hmm.  As I see it, here are the assumptions that are being made:

* The brain is describable (modelable).  That is, we can write down
  a description of the brain that is sufficient to allow a computer
  to produce the same behaviors when operating on the description [the
  real interesting issue is how we determine whether the behavior
  is sufficiently similar; more on this some other day]

* The behavior of neurons is an appropriate framework in which to
  write this description.  First approximations treat the interaction
  between neurons as being done through synapses, because that appears
  to be the most important issue.  Interactions caused by chemicals
  transported by diffusive mechanisms are also necessary, but present
  no tremendous analytical difficulties -- perhaps with the exception
  of the modification of neural function caused by these chemicals
  (and the signals sent via synapses too) -- but in any case, the point
  is that the possible interactions can be enumerated (including the
  other interactions with the brain's "environment" which seem to be
  even more statistical in nature), which allows us to reduce brain modeling
  to neuron modeling.  We then become interested in the requirements for
  this neuron model (or, more realistically, class of neuron models as
  they differ greatly).

Note that this is a THEORY.  When we "confidently assume that these
assumptions are correct", go light on the "confidently".  Further
analysis does largely depend on the theory being true, but so what?
Just prepend "If current theory is correct..." to all such speculative
analysis.

Your comments about linear summation and phase and other complexities
are very interesting, but are not really criticisms of the assumptions
noted above.  Few people really believe that the simple models used
today by people developing "neurocomputing" are appropriate for use
in uploading-as-simulation-of-brain-function!

This difference is reflected in the observation that there are two
types of neural network researchers (who seldom have a lot to say
to each other):

 * Those who are attempting to build effective regression, clustering,
   or other computational mechanisms from massively parallel computing
   units (inspired by neurons in the dim past).
 
 * Those who are trying to model the subtleties of real neural networks
   and understand their behavior.

It's easy to tell them apart by the amount of biological detail they
include in their papers.  Paul, which kind are you?

Anyway, the issue for us here is how difficult the modeling is likely
to be -- because it's convenient we say "how many bits to specify
each neuron/synapse/whatever?"  Educated guesses of this let us
play scenarios about technological requirements, as well as let us
reflect on what we can actually expect from our current machines;
comments of "it's the software, stupid" are perhaps inappropriate if
reasonable neuron models can only simulate tiny networks on current
hardware.

Also note that whenever a researcher sets out to develop a model of
neural function, s/he is implicitly doing exactly the same sort of
guesswork that we're talking about here -- by choosing the level of
description and complexity of the model that they propose and study.
All we're doing is expressing that guesswork in a different way.

derek


