Perry Metzger writes:
> [There are] activities I've dropped doing because they are time-sinks
> -- watching TV, for example. I haven't watched any TV or even owned a
> set in about four years. I'm a lot happier for it, by the way.

Same here.  My wife and I have been tubeless for almost five years now
(not as an act of volition - movers broke our old one and we never got
around to replacing it) and feel the better for it.  One side-effect
though: we both find ourselves highly averse to linear, noninteractive
entertainment (i.e., straight TV or movie viewing), especially if it
monopolizes the time it occupies.  I feel this effect particularly
strongly, as I've been working full-time while going for my Ph.D., and
thus want maximum return on my leisure time.  Watching a first-run movie,
sitting still in the dark while entertainment is beamed relentlessly into
my brain, is now almost always unpleasant; ditto the theater.  I find I
want multiple inputs instead; for instance, my idea of a fun time these
days is to make a large newsstand run, then repair to a favorite open-air
bar to peruse the results, thus simultaneously reading, drinking, basking,
conversing, and peoplewatching if all goes right.

To put this into a relevant context: Is this a unique quirk of mine, or is
it more widespread? If so, will it get more common as the pace of life
picks up?  What sort of multi-tasking recreational environments are we likely
to see evolve for high-intensity loafing - dare we say "transleisure"?  

